          "our_approach": "Recommend a phased, buy-not-build rollout sized to HFB's actual network (18 counties, 1,600+ partners, mixed dry/refrigerated freight with 47% of volume being time-sensitive produce): (1) Baseline instrumentation — GPS/telematics on the existing fleet for 60-90 days to establish real miles-per-route, stop counts, and on-time delivery rate, since no such baseline is currently public [addresses the open gap below]; (2) Pilot a commercial VRP route-optimization platform (the same category of tool FedEx bought via RouteSmart rather than building in-house) on one or two counties first, not a system-wide rollout, mirroring how UPS itself phased ORION over 2015-2016 rather than a single big-bang deployment; (3) Build refrigerated-capacity and perishability time-windows into the routing constraints from day one — this is the one dimension where HFB's problem differs materially from UPS/Amazon/FedEx parcel routing, since 85M lbs of the network's volume (produce) has spoilage deadlines that a generic least-distance VRP would ignore; (4) Report realized vs. projected savings separately and explicitly, the same distinction UPS draws between its confirmed 6-8 mi/route figure and its 100M-mile deployment projection — this avoids overstating HFB-specific gains before the pilot produces real numbers; (5) Expand county-by-county only after each pilot region's realized numbers are in hand."
